## Why This Is Asked

Tests knowledge of bit-twiddling tricks for 128-bit values without loops.

## Key Concepts

- SWAR/popcount for 64-bit chunks
- Safe 128-bit aggregation without branches

## Code Example

```c
uint32_t popcount128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo){
  auto count64 = [](uint64_t v){
    v = v - ((v>>1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
    v = (v & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((v>>2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
    v = (v + (v>>4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
    v = v + (v>>8);
    v = v + (v>>16);
    v = v + (v>>32);
    return (uint64_t)(v & 0x7F);
  };
  uint64_t c1 = count64(hi);
  uint64_t c2 = count64(lo);
  return (uint32_t)(c1 + c2);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this to a 256-bit value?
- What are the risks of using this in performance-critical hot paths compared to builtin intrinsics?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to manipulate a 128-bit value by composing 64-bit operations, enforcing a non-loop, fixed-sequence approach for performance.

## Key Concepts
- Endianness and byte-level reversal
- Bit-twiddling for 64-bit reversal
- Combining two 64-bit halves into a 128-bit result

## Code Example
```javascript
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;
static inline uint64_t reverse64(uint64_t v) {
  v = ((v & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) << 8) | ((v >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL);
  v = ((v & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) << 16) | ((v >> 16) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL);
  v = (v << 32) | (v >> 32);
  return v;
}
uint128 swapEndian128(uint128 x) {
  uint128 r;
  r.lo = reverse64(x.hi);
  r.hi = reverse64(x.lo);
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt if only a single 128-bit type isn’t available?
- How would you validate correctness with representative test patterns (all zeros, all ones, alternating bits)?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to implement conditional data movement without branching. It demonstrates how to construct a full-word mask from a single flag and apply it consistently across 128-bit halves.

## Key Concepts
- **Branchless conditioning via masks:** Using arithmetic to convert a boolean into an all-0s or all-1s mask
- **128-bit representation:** A 128-bit value split into hi/lo 64-bit halves
- **Portable unsigned arithmetic:** Safe, well-defined behavior across platforms

## Code Example
```c
uint128 swapHalvesIf128(uint128 x, unsigned c) {
    unsigned m = -(unsigned)(c != 0);
    uint64_t hi = (x.hi & ~m) | (x.lo & m);
    uint64_t lo = (x.lo & ~m) | (x.hi & m);
    return (uint128){.hi = hi, .lo = lo};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason about cross-boundary bit interactions and implement constant-time checks using only bitwise ops. 

## Key Concepts
- Bitwise shifts and masks across multiword values
- Detecting adjacent bits within each 64-bit half and across the 64-bit boundary
- Branchless boolean results for interview clarity

## Code Example
```javascript
int hasAdjacentOnes128(uint128 x) {
  uint64_t a = x.lo & (x.lo << 1);
  uint64_t b = x.hi & (x.hi << 1);
  int cross = ((x.lo >> 63) & 1) & (x.hi & 1);
  return (a | b | cross) != 0;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this to detect a run of exactly k adjacent ones? 
- How would you implement a similar check for 256-bit values represented as two uint128s?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests low-level bitboard thinking, edge masking, and 128-bit manipulation without loops.

## Key Concepts
- 8x16 bitboard mapping in 128 bits (bit i = row*16 + col).
- Horizontal moves use 1-bit shifts with edge masks (COL0/COL15).
- Vertical moves use 16-bit shifts with TOP/BOTTOM masks.

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode close to C for clarity
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;
uint128 expandMask128(uint128 m){
  uint128 left  = (m & ~COL0) >> 1;
  uint128 right = (m & ~COL15) << 1;
  uint128 up    = (m & ~TOP) << 16;
  uint128 down  = (m & ~BOTTOM) >> 16;
  return or128(or128(left, right), or128(up, down));
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for an irregular 3x5 tile grid embedded in 128 bits?
- What are the performance implications of branching-free bitboard ops on modern CPUs?

## Why This Is Asked

Demonstrates capability to manipulate multi-word integers with pure bit tricks, a common requirement in crypto and low-level systems programming. It tests cross-lane thinking and branch-free implementation.

## Key Concepts

- Cross-lane bit propagation between hi and lo.
- Branch-free 128-bit rotation using only shifts and OR.

## Code Example

```c
uint128 rotateLeft1281(uint128 x) {
  uint128 r;
  r.lo = (x.lo << 1) | (x.hi >> 63);
  r.hi = (x.hi << 1) | (x.lo >> 63);
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you generalize to rotate left by k (0 <= k < 128) without branches?
- How would you test boundary cases like k=0, k=127, across hi/lo boundaries?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests endianness handling and 128-bit manipulation with no branches.

## Key Concepts
- 64-bit byte reversal
- 128-bit composition
- Branchless bit-twiddling

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;
static inline uint64_t reverseBytes64(uint64_t x) {
  x = ((x & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) << 8) | ((x >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL);
  x = ((x & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) << 16) | ((x >> 16) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL);
  x = (x << 32) | (x >> 32);
  return x;
}
uint128 mirrorBytes128(uint128 x) {
  uint128 y; y.hi = reverseBytes64(x.lo); y.lo = reverseBytes64(x.hi); return y;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for streaming data where only a portion is available?
- What are the performance trade-offs of inlining reverseBytes64?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of compact bit-twiddling techniques to count bits without loops, a common interview staple for performance-critical code.

## Key Concepts

- Parallel bit counting (SIMD-style within a register)
- Bit masks and shifts for aggregation
- Portability across unsigned integers and compilers

## Code Example

```c
uint32_t popcount32(uint32_t n) {
  n = n - ((n >> 1) & 0x55555555);
  n = (n & 0x33333333) + ((n >> 2) & 0x33333333);
  n = (n + (n >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F;
  return (n * 0x01010101) >> 24;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend to 64-bit?  
- What tests would you add to verify edge cases (all zeros, all ones, random patterns)?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests low-level bit manipulation and the ability to express 128-bit results without loops.

## Key Concepts

- Bit interleaving (Morton code)
- Bit spreading with masks
- 128-bit composition from two 64-bit halves

## Code Example

```javascript
function interleave64to128(a,b){
  // spreading, combining, returning {hi, lo}
  // exact spread64to128 implementation omitted for brevity
  const spreadA = spread64to128(a);
  const spreadB = spread64to128(b);
  const hi = spreadA.hi | (spreadB.hi << 1n);
  const lo = spreadA.lo | (spreadB.lo << 1n);
  return {hi, lo};
}
```

